  "result": "The rule and its witnesses for NRS_484B_270_7a are written, and the gate returned **OK** on the second of its 6 allowed runs.\n\n- **Gate runs:** the first run was a slip on the untouched skeleton and returned `UNUSED_CLAIM`. I also ran one extra shell command by mistake; the permission layer denied it, so it never executed.\n- **Who the rule applies to:** riders of a bicycle, an electric bicycle or an electric scooter, as defined in the three legal definitions the node names.\n- **What it forbids:** at any moment, a moving motor vehicle that has the rider ahead of it, braking at 4.0 m/s² or harder, while the two would pass within 3 m of each other in the next 3 s.\n- **Narrowings:** none of my own.\n\n**Two witnesses differ from the node's narratives**, both because of how the run's reading classifies road users:\n- **`idle`:** the narrative says \"a car\", but the reading leaves cars unresolved under the electric-bicycle definition. A car as subject would make the rule's applicability unknown rather than not applicable, so I used a moped. A moped falls under none of the three definitions.\n- **`blind`:** the narrative says \"an electric scooter\", but I used an electric bicycle. Under the reading, no road user is ever definitely an electric scooter, so a scooter subject would come out unknown even with nothing hidden. With an e-bike, hiding the car's braking is what makes the verdict unknown, which is what this witness has to show.\n\n**For the plan agent:** a consequence of the scooter point is that this rule can never return a decided verdict for an electric-scooter rider; the best it can give is unknown. That should be checked if scooter riders are meant to be judged.\n\n`task_result.md` in the working directory has the run log, these choices and the reason for finishing.",
